## Disaster recovery

Solo DR is git-driven and intentionally minimal:

1. Clone the repo to a new machine.
2. Run `mtix init` if `.mtix/data/mtix.db` is missing.
3. Run `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json` to rebuild the SQLite store from
   the last committed snapshot.
4. Verify with `mtix list --status open`, and `mtix show <id>` on a task
   you know has comments.

From mtix 0.5.4 the snapshot carries every field of every task, comments
(annotations) and the activity stream included (`schema_version` 2.0.0),
so the restore brings them back. A snapshot committed by an earlier
version has no comments, and a restore from it has none either.

Every machine that shares the snapshot must run 0.5.4 or later. An
earlier version skips a 2.0.0 snapshot as newer than it supports, but its
next writing command re-exports `.mtix/tasks.json` from its own store,
without the comments and changes it skipped; committing that file reverts
them. On an earlier version even an `mtix import` that fails rewrites
`.mtix/tasks.json` the same way. Until every machine is upgraded, run no
writing commands or `mtix import` on an older one and do not commit its
`.mtix/tasks.json`. If one was committed, restore `.mtix/tasks.json` from
the last good commit in git history, or restore the store from the backup
taken before the import (`.mtix/data/backups/pre-sync-<time>.db`, the
newest 5 kept; `.mtix/data/pre-sync-backup.db` on 0.5.3 and earlier). A
0.5.4 store is protected by the auto-import guard: a snapshot that lacks
comments, activity, tasks or field values the store holds is not
imported, and nothing changes (see "Auto-import refused" above).

Automatic import is on by default. `mtix config set sync.auto_sync false`
turns it off (the setting is in the tracked `.mtix/config.yaml`, so every
clone gets it, but an empty store, such as a fresh clone's, is always
imported). Writes never overwrite a snapshot that changed on disk and was
not imported: with the switch off, import a pulled snapshot yourself
(`mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode merge`), or keep your board with
`mtix sync --fix`. The merge decides per task: when the task's content
(title, description, prompt, acceptance, labels) matches the file's, your
field values win, and otherwise the file's copy wins, undoing your edits
to that task; afterwards check `git log -p .mtix/tasks.json` and reapply
what it undid.

For richer DR (sync hub backup + immutable cold storage), see the
[safety-critical workflow](safety-critical.md), which uses the BYO
Postgres sync hub and documents the full restore procedure.

